From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 21:11:02 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: define <app> and <webview> as elements the fork
 owns

A custom element's name must contain a hyphen, per spec, so the tags the shells
are written against -- <app> and <webview> -- cannot be customElements.define()d
at all. They have to be elements Blink defines, the way Electron's <webview>
was. That is what this does.

<app> is the design's element, arrived at properly instead of through a canvas:
HTMLAppElement owns a SurfaceLayerBridge and an ExternalSurfaceEmbedder, and
LayoutAppSurface -- a LayoutReplaced -- records its cc::SurfaceLayer as a
foreign layer, which is what puts a window in the page's own property trees.
app-id is a reflected content attribute, the way <img src> is, so setting it in
markup and setting it from script are one operation. The layout box is the
configure: LayoutAppSurface::UpdateAfterLayout reports the pixel-snapped box and
the element re-embeds at the new size, ignoring layouts that did not move it.

<webview> is a frame owner, so the nested browsing context is the one <iframe>
creates and process allocation, site isolation and navigation are Chromium's.
It reports FrameOwnerElementType::kIframe rather than a value of its own,
because everything that switches on the type wants to treat it as an iframe and
adding a case to a mojom enum shared with //content would put the fork in every
one of those switches for no behaviour it wants to differ.

Two gaps in <webview>, both stated in the header rather than discovered later:
X-Frame-Options and CSP frame-ancestors apply, because a frame owner is a
frame, so a site that refuses framing refuses to load -- that is the one thing
Electron's guest-view <webview> bought that this does not. And goBack()/
goForward() reach the nested context's History directly, so they work only
while it is in this process; driving history across a process boundary is the
browser's to do and is not done here.

The generated lists this touches are the cost the design avoided by putting
embedExternalSurface() on <canvas>. Paying it was the decision; the tags are
what shells write.

New files are untracked and live in the series' src/.
